# .easignore — what the EAS cloud build receives.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it, EAS falls back to .gitignore, and
# deenapp/.gitignore contains a bare `content/` rule plus `assets/content.zip`.
# The content pack is therefore excluded from the upload, and the cloud build's
# `npm ci` runs scripts/unpack-content.mjs as postinstall, finds none of its
# markers, tries the two historical download URLs (both dead since pass 34d),
# and THROWS — failing the build about twenty minutes in with
# "unpack-content: content pack unavailable".
#
# The pack is already extracted in the working tree (114 surahs, 15 hadith .gz,
# 14 meta, 4 islamic files = the 147 require()s in src/lib/content.ts), so the
# correct behaviour is to UPLOAD IT and let postinstall no-op on its markers.
# That is what this file does: it keeps the genuinely unnecessary things out
# (dependencies, tool state, generated native projects) and lets assets/content
# through.
#
# Verified before adding: all 7 MARKERS in scripts/unpack-content.mjs exist
# under assets/content/, so an uploaded tree makes postinstall a no-op instead
# of a download attempt.

# dependencies — EAS runs its own install in the cloud
node_modules/
.npm/
.yarn/

# tool state
.expo/
.metro-health-check*
*.tsbuildinfo
npm-debug.*
yarn-error.*

# generated native projects — prebuild regenerates these from app.json
/ios/
/android/

# build output
dist/
dist-app/
dist-root/
web-build/
coverage/

# logs and OS noise
*.log
.DS_Store

# local env and tokens — never upload
.env*.local
.expo-token
.token

# NOTE: assets/content/ is deliberately NOT listed. It is ~20 MB and must reach
# the cloud build. assets/content.zip stays absent; the extracted tree is the
# source of truth now.
